1
00:00:03,381 --> 00:00:05,617
Kakushi uses Privy in two places.

2
00:00:05,657 --> 00:00:07,614
In the app, Privy is the sign-in:

3
00:00:07,654 --> 00:00:10,067
email, Google or any wallet.

4
00:00:11,935 --> 00:00:13,276
On the operator side,

5
00:00:13,316 --> 00:00:16,051
a Maker's hot wallet can be a Privy server wallet,

6
00:00:16,091 --> 00:00:19,503
fenced by a policy that denies everything by default.

7
00:00:19,946 --> 00:00:23,161
This is a real run on Monad testnet.

8
00:00:23,244 --> 00:00:26,431
Privy signs the one kind of thing the policy allows,

9
00:00:26,471 --> 00:00:31,067
a USDC approve to the PayoutRouter, and it lands on chain.

10
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:34,431
Then it refuses to move the USDC anywhere else:

11
00:00:34,471 --> 00:00:36,106
policy violation.

12
00:00:36,458 --> 00:00:40,354
The policy allows only fills and refunds through the router,

13
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:44,243
dispute answers, approvals to the router or the margin

14
00:00:44,283 --> 00:00:47,381
contract, and the Maker's own setup.

15
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:52,141
A compromised Maker host can't drain inventory or margin.

16
00:00:52,758 --> 00:00:55,574
The approve is on chain, sent from the Privy wallet.

17
00:00:55,614 --> 00:00:58,906
One honest note: the live Maker 1 still signs with its own

18
00:00:58,946 --> 00:01:02,209
key; the Privy wallet runs this fenced path.

